To model this many-to-many relationship (via a join table) in **DBML (Database Markup Language)**, and ensure the generated `User` class has a list of `Role` objects, you need to define the schema using the correct DBML syntax. Here's how you can do it:

---

### ✅ DBML Schema for Many-to-Many User-Role Relationship

```dbml
Table User {
  UserId int [pk]
}

Table RoleUserXRef {
  RoleUserId int [pk]
  RoleId int
  UserId int
}

Table Role {
  RoleId int [pk]
  Name string
}

Ref: User.UserId < RoleUserXRef.UserId
Ref: Role.RoleId < RoleUserXRef.RoleId
```

---

### 📌 Explanation

- **User** has a `UserId` primary key.
- **RoleUserXRef** is the join table (also called an association table) with a composite primary key (`RoleUserId`), and foreign keys to `User.UserId` and `Role.RoleId`.
- **Role** has a `RoleId` primary key and a `Name`.

By setting up the references this way, when you generate classes from this DBML (using tools like [dbdiagram.io](https://dbdiagram.io/) or [LinqToDB](https://github.com/linq2db/linq2db)), the `User` class will have a collection of `Role` objects.

---

### 🧩 Generated Class Structure (Conceptual)

When you generate classes from this DBML, you’ll get something like this:

```csharp
public class User {
    public int UserId { get; set; }
    public List<Role> Roles { get; set; }  // This is automatically generated
}

public class Role {
    public int RoleId { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public List<User> Users { get; set; }  // Optional, for the reverse relationship
}

public class RoleUserXRef {
    public int RoleUserId { get; set; }
    public int RoleId { get; set; }
    public int UserId { get; set; }

    // Navigation properties
    public Role Role { get; set; }
    public User User { get; set; }
}
```

---

### 🛠 Tips

- If you're using a tool like **dbdiagram.io**, you can paste the DBML code and visualize the schema.
- If you're using **LinqToDB**, it will generate the appropriate associations and allow you to query `User.Roles` directly.
- If you're using **Entity Framework**, the structure is similar, and EF will infer the many-to-many relationship if the join table is named correctly (e.g., `UserRoles`), but in your case, you have a custom name (`RoleUserXRef`), so you may need to configure it manually in `OnModelCreating`.

