**Solution Explanation**

For every student name `s` and every pseudonym `p`

```
lcp(s , p) = length of the longest common prefix of s and p
```

The quality of a complete assignment (one pseudonym for every student,
every pseudonym used exactly once) is

```
quality = Σ lcp( student i , pseudonym matched with i )
```

`n ≤ 100 000`, the total length of all 2·n strings is at most `800 000`.

We have to output

* the maximum possible quality
* one assignment that reaches this maximum



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 1.  Observations

* If two strings share a prefix of length `k`,
  every pair that contains both of them has `lcp ≥ k`.
* The deeper a common prefix is, the larger the contribution of a pair
  that uses this prefix.
* Therefore we would like to pair as many strings as possible
  **at the deepest possible common prefixes**.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 2.  Trie

All names (students + pseudonyms) are inserted into one trie.

```
node
 ├─ children[26]   (indices of child nodes, -1 = none)
 ├─ depth           (length of the prefix represented by this node)
 ├─ students        (indices of student leaves in the subtree)
 └─ pseudonyms      (indices of pseudonym leaves in the subtree)
```

*The leaf of a string is the node that represents the whole string.*

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 3.  Greedy matching on the trie

We process the trie **bottom‑up** (post‑order).

For a node `u`

```
1.  merge all students and pseudonyms coming from its children
    into the two vectors of node u
    (small‑to‑large merge – each index moves only O(log n) times)

2.  while both vectors are non‑empty
        take one student and one pseudonym
        pair them
        add u.depth to the total quality
```

Why is this optimal?

*Every pair that shares a prefix of length `d` can be matched
only at a node whose depth is `d` or smaller.*
If we postpone a possible match to an ancestor,
the depth (and therefore the contribution) can only become smaller.
Thus matching as many pairs as possible at the deepest node
is always safe – it never hurts any other pair.

Processing all nodes guarantees that every string is matched exactly once,
and the obtained quality is maximal.

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 4.  Algorithm

```
build trie from all 2n strings
post‑order traversal of the trie (iterative, no recursion depth problems)
    for every node u in post‑order
        merge children into u
        match as many pairs as possible at u
output total quality and all pairs
```

--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 5.  Correctness Proof  

We prove that the algorithm outputs a maximum quality assignment.

---

##### Lemma 1  
For a fixed node `u` (depth `d`) the algorithm matches
`min( number_of_students_in_subtree(u) ,
      number_of_pseudonyms_in_subtree(u) )`
pairs at `u`, each contributing `d` to the quality.

**Proof.**

All students and pseudonyms in the subtree of `u`
are stored in the two vectors of `u` after all children were merged.
The algorithm repeatedly removes one element from each vector
until one of them becomes empty.
The number of removals equals the minimum of the two sizes,
hence the lemma. ∎



##### Lemma 2  
Let `T` be the set of all pairs produced by the algorithm.
For every node `u` the sum of `lcp` values of pairs in `T`
that are matched at or below `u` equals

```
Σ depth(v) * matched_pairs_at(v)   over all descendants v of u
```

**Proof.**

Pairs matched at a node `v` contribute exactly `depth(v)` to the sum
(Lemma&nbsp;1).  
No pair matched at a descendant of `v` contributes to `v` again.
Summing over all descendants gives the stated expression. ∎



##### Lemma 3  
Let `O` be any optimal assignment.
For every node `u` the number of pairs of `O`
that are matched at or below `u` is **at most**
the number of pairs matched by the algorithm at or below `u`.

**Proof.**

Consider node `u`.  
All pairs of `O` that are matched at or below `u`
must use only strings from the subtree of `u`.
The algorithm matches at node `u` as many pairs as possible
(Lemma&nbsp;1).  
Therefore no other assignment can match more pairs
inside the subtree of `u`. ∎



##### Lemma 4  
The total quality produced by the algorithm is **not smaller**
than the total quality of any optimal assignment.

**Proof.**

Let `P(v)` be the number of pairs matched by the algorithm at node `v`.  
Let `Q(v)` be the number of pairs matched by an optimal assignment at node `v`.

From Lemma&nbsp;3, for every node `v`  
`Q(v) ≤ P(v)`.

The quality of the algorithm is

```
Σ depth(v) * P(v)
```

and the quality of any optimal assignment is

```
Σ depth(v) * Q(v)
```

Since all depths are non‑negative and `P(v) ≥ Q(v)` for every `v`,
the first sum is not smaller than the second. ∎



##### Theorem  
The algorithm outputs a complete assignment with maximum possible
quality.

**Proof.**

The algorithm produces exactly `n` pairs – one for every student
and one for every pseudonym – hence it is a complete assignment.

By Lemma&nbsp;4 its quality is at least as large as the quality of any
other assignment, i.e. it is optimal. ∎



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 6.  Complexity Analysis

*`N` – number of strings (`2n`),  
`L` – total length of all strings (`≤ 800 000`).*

*Building the trie*  
`O(L)` time, `O(L)` nodes.

*Small‑to‑large merging*  
Each string index is moved only `O(log n)` times  
⇒ `O(n log n)` time.

*Matching*  
Every pair is processed once  
⇒ `O(n)` time.

*Total time*   `O(L + n log n)`  (≤ about 2·10⁶ operations).

*Memory*  
Trie nodes: `L` nodes, each has 26 integers (children) + depth + two vectors.  
`≈ 8·10⁵ · 26 · 4  ≈ 83 MB` plus the vectors of indices (`2n` integers).  
Fits easily into the usual 512 MB limit.



--------------------------------------------------------------------

#### 7.  Reference Implementation  (GNU‑C++17)

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct Node {
    int child[26];
    int depth;
    vector<int> students;   // indices of students in the subtree
    vector<int> pseudonyms; // indices of pseudonyms in the subtree
    Node(int d = 0) : depth(d) {
        fill(begin(child), end(child), -1);
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;
    vector<string> students(n + 1), pseudonyms(n + 1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) cin >> students[i];
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) cin >> pseudonyms[i];

    vector<Node> trie;
    trie.reserve(800000 + 5);
    trie.emplace_back(0);          // root

    auto insert = [&](const string &s, int idx, bool isStudent) {
        int node = 0;
        for (char ch : s) {
            int c = ch - 'a';
            if (trie[node].child[c] == -1) {
                trie[node].child[c] = (int)trie.size();
                trie.emplace_back(trie[node].depth + 1);
            }
            node = trie[node].child[c];
        }
        if (isStudent) trie[node].students.push_back(idx);
        else           trie[node].pseudonyms.push_back(idx);
    };

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) insert(students[i], i, true);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) insert(pseudonyms[i], i, false);

    /* iterative post‑order traversal */
    vector<int> order;
    order.reserve(trie.size());
    vector<pair<int,int>> st; // node, next child index to visit
    st.emplace_back(0, 0);
    while (!st.empty()) {
        auto &top = st.back();
        int u = top.first;
        int &i = top.second;
        if (i < 26) {
            int v = trie[u].child[i];
            ++i;
            if (v != -1) st.emplace_back(v, 0);
        } else {
            order.push_back(u);
            st.pop_back();
        }
    }

    long long total = 0;
    vector<pair<int,int>> answer;
    answer.reserve(n);

    for (int u : order) {
        Node &node = trie[u];
        // merge all children into this node
        for (int c = 0; c < 26; ++c) {
            int v = node.child[c];
            if (v == -1) continue;
            Node &child = trie[v];

            // merge students
            if (node.students.size() < child.students.size())
                swap(node.students, child.students);
            node.students.insert(node.students.end(),
                                 child.students.begin(),
                                 child.students.end());
            child.students.clear();

            // merge pseudonyms
            if (node.pseudonyms.size() < child.pseudonyms.size())
                swap(node.pseudonyms, child.pseudonyms);
            node.pseudonyms.insert(node.pseudonyms.end(),
                                   child.pseudonyms.begin(),
                                   child.pseudonyms.end());
            child.pseudonyms.clear();
        }

        // match as many as possible at this node
        while (!node.students.empty() && !node.pseudonyms.empty()) {
            int s = node.students.back(); node.students.pop_back();
            int p = node.pseudonyms.back(); node.pseudonyms.pop_back();
            answer.emplace_back(s, p);
            total += node.depth;
        }
    }

    cout << total << '\n';
    for (auto [s, p] : answer) {
        cout << s << ' ' << p << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

The program follows exactly the algorithm proven correct above
and is fully compliant with the GNU++17 compiler.

