**(a)  Required HNO₃ and Cu**

The reaction of copper with dilute nitric acid is

\[
\mathrm{Cu(s)+4\,HNO_3(aq)\;\longrightarrow\;Cu(NO_3)_2(aq)+2\,NO(g)+2\,H_2O(l)}
\]

* Moles of Cu(NO₃)₂ needed  
\[
n_{\mathrm{Cu(NO_3)_2}} = M \times V = 0.500\;\text{mol L}^{-1}\times 1.50\;\text{L}=0.750\;\text{mol}
\]

* Because the stoichiometry is 1 Cu : 1 Cu(NO₃)₂, the same number of moles of Cu is required:  
\[
n_{\mathrm{Cu}} = 0.750\;\text{mol}
\]

* Moles of HNO₃ needed (4 mol per 1 mol Cu)  
\[
n_{\mathrm{HNO_3}} = 4 \times 0.750 = 3.00\;\text{mol}
\]

* Volume of 6.00 M HNO₃ that contains 3.00 mol:  
\[
V_{\mathrm{HNO_3}} = \frac{n_{\mathrm{HNO_3}}}{C} = \frac{3.00\;\text{mol}}{6.00\;\text{mol L}^{-1}} = 0.500\;\text{L}
\]

* Mass of copper required  
\[
m_{\mathrm{Cu}} = n_{\mathrm{Cu}}\times M_{\mathrm{Cu}}
= 0.750\;\text{mol}\times 63.55\;\text{g mol}^{-1}
= 47.7\;\text{g}
\]

\[
\boxed{V_{\mathrm{HNO_3}} = 0.500\;\text{L}\qquad m_{\mathrm{Cu}} = 47.7\;\text{g}}
\]

---

**(b)  Volume of NO produced**

From the stoichiometry, 1 mol Cu gives 2 mol NO.  
\[
n_{\mathrm{NO}} = 2 \times 0.750 = 1.50\;\text{mol}
\]

The NO is collected over water at 745 torr and 18.0 °C.  
Saturation vapor pressure of water at 18 °C ≈ 14.8 torr.

\[
P_{\mathrm{NO}} = P_{\text{total}}-P_{\mathrm{H_2O}}
= 745\;\text{torr} - 14.8\;\text{torr}
= 730.2\;\text{torr}
\]

Convert to atmospheres:
\[
P_{\mathrm{NO}} = \frac{730.2}{760}\;\text{atm}=0.9608\;\text{atm}
\]

Use the ideal‑gas law ( \(R = 0.082057\;\text{L atm mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}\) ):

\[
V_{\mathrm{NO}} = \frac{nRT}{P}
= \frac{(1.50\;\text{mol})(0.082057\;\text{L atm mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1})(291.15\;\text{K})}
{0.9608\;\text{atm}}
= 37.3\;\text{L}
\]

\[
\boxed{V_{\mathrm{NO}} \approx 37\;\text{L}}
\]

(If one neglects the water vapor pressure, the volume would be ≈ 36.6 L; the difference is small but the problem explicitly states “collected over water,” so the corrected value is used.)

