&lt;p&gt;Consider a sequence of numbers generated by the function $u_n = n^4$: $1, 16, 81, 256, 625, 1296, \dots$&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If we are given the first $k$ terms of this sequence, it is not possible to accurately predict the value of the next term, as there are infinitely many polynomial functions that can model the sequence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For instance, if we were only given the first two terms of this sequence, assuming a linear relationship would be the simplest approach, and we would predict the next term to be $31$ (common difference $15$). Even if we were presented with the first three terms, by the same principle of simplicity, a quadratic relationship should be assumed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We shall define $\operatorname{OP}(k, n)$ to be the $n$&lt;sup&gt;th&lt;/sup&gt; term of the optimum polynomial generating function for the first $k$ terms of a sequence. It should be clear that $\operatorname{OP}(k, n)$ will accurately generate the terms of the sequence for $n \le k$, and potentially the &lt;dfn&gt;first incorrect term&lt;/dfn&gt; (FIT) will be $\operatorname{OP}(k, k+1)$; in which case we shall call it a &lt;dfn&gt;bad OP&lt;/dfn&gt; (BOP).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As a basis, if we were only given the first term of sequence, it would be most sensible to assume constancy; that is, for $n \ge 2$, $\operatorname{OP}(1, n) = u_1$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hence we obtain the following $\operatorname{OP}$s for the quartic sequence:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;margin_left&#34;&gt;
&lt;table&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;$\operatorname{OP}(1, n) = 1$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1, {\color{red}\mathbf 1}, 1, 1, \dots$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;$\operatorname{OP}(2, n) = 15n - 14$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1, 16, {\color{red}\mathbf{31}}, \dots$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;$\operatorname{OP}(3, n) = 40n^2 - 59n + 20$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1, 16, 81, {\color{red}\mathbf{202}}, \dots$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;$\operatorname{OP}(4, n) = n^4$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;$1, 16, 81, 256, 625, \dots$&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Clearly no BOPs exist for $k \ge 4$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;By considering the sum of FITs generated by the BOPs (indicated in &lt;span class=&#34;red&#34;&gt;&lt;b&gt;red&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt; above), we obtain $1 + 31 + 202 = 234$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consider the following eleventh degree polynomial generating function:
$$u_n = 1 - n + n^2 - n^3 + n^4 - n^5 + n^6 - n^7 + n^8 - n^9 + n^{10} - n^{11}.$$&lt;/p&gt;
